{
  "name": "φ⁴ oscillons",
  "description": "Klein–Gordon with a double-well (φ⁴) potential: the vacua are u = −1 and u = +1. Bubbles of the +1 vacuum collapse, and instead of vanishing they settle into oscillons: lumps that pulse for a very long time, shedding only a little radiation. Domain walls between the vacua are kinks that straighten and shrink. The interior is conservative (no friction): radiation shed by the collapsing bubbles would circle the wrap-around field forever, so a sponge border (gamma ramping up over the outer ~28 px) soaks it up, as an infinite domain would let it escape. A small viscosity nu damps only grid-scale ripples; set nu = 0 for purely reversible dynamics. Paint gamma to add or remove absorbers.",
  "rule": "builtin:5",
  "size": 256,
  "start": "initial",
